  "structural_conclusion": "Existing snapshots prove substantial free-list bytes below order10 but at/above order6. They support guest high-order coverage limitation as a distinct hypothesis, not resident-tail starvation or host benefit.",
  "limits": [
    "Buddy/pagetype snapshots are from earlier V6 guests, not the V7 positive/tail PID/boot or the same probe phase; never transplant their byte totals to V7.",
    "Counts are existing free-list blocks, include PageReported blocks and CMA, lack physical-address identity, PCP coverage, zone-watermark state, host exposure and PRESENT correlation; structural candidate upper bounds only.",
    "A lower minimum includes smaller existing buddy blocks without compacting memory, touching guest pages, splitting high-order blocks, or gathering subchunk ownership. Existing4MiB SG tails remain.",
    "Order6 is256KiB at guest4KiB and aligns with fixed host256KiB objects for the observed main-RAM base0x40000000. Host16KiB is a separate page size. Order0–5 smallest SGs fail whole-chunk eligibility.",
    "Lower order reduces additional zone watermark128MiB(order10)/64MiB(order9) to8MiB(order6), increases eligible free notifications/list work/SG count;32-SG capacity/2s scheduling delay remain, no latency or memory-saving bound inferred.",
    "For equal bytes represented entirely by minimum-sized blocks, order6 has16x descriptor count vsorder10 (8x vsorder9); actual kernel does not split existing high-order blocks, so this is a volume ratio, not measured traffic.",
    "Host retains2attempts/32inspections perSG; smaller whole SGs may expose different ranges and improve attempted-byte fraction, but more SGs/BHs and unchanged expensive caller validation require measured shared-budget assessment.",
